TEXT -304 1040 Left 2 ;FINAL 1 - OUTPUT FOR EXAMPLE INPUT SIGNALS (transient).   Set  sig = 1..5  and  kAC = 0 (DC coupling) or 1 (AC coupling), then Run.\nPlot V(in) (the BNC input) and V(v_out) (the ADC pin). Gain and stop time are picked automatically from sig:\n  sig=1  sine 1 kHz, 2 V amplitude            G=20   (small signal, highest gain)\n  sig=2  sine 1 MHz, 40 V amplitude           G=1    (top of the band, large signal)\n  sig=3  square 1 kHz, +-40 V, 1 us edges     G=1    (shows the divider compensation: flat top = OK)\n  sig=4  logic square 100 kHz, 0..5 V         G=10   (signal with a DC part: DC mode keeps it, AC mode removes it)\n  sig=5  12 V DC + 1 V sine 10 kHz (ripple)   G=3 in DC mode, G=20 in AC mode  (AC mode removes the 12 V, so more gain can be used)\nAll sources start at their mean value, so the AC-coupling capacitor is already charged (no 0.5 s settling to wait for).\nRdac/Vdac = DAC offset trim (see FINAL 3). The BAT54S clamp is not modelled: above 3.3 V the real pin is clamped.
